---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { loadGeAssumptions, loadGeBacktest, loadGeSummary } from "../../../lib/predictions";
import { loadGeWinProbability } from "../../../lib/geWinProbability.js";

const a = loadGeAssumptions();
const b = loadGeBacktest();
const geSummary = loadGeSummary();
const wp = loadGeWinProbability();
const params = a?.parameters || {};
const pct0 = (x: number | undefined) => (typeof x === "number" ? `${Math.round(x * 100)}%` : "n/a");

// Defensive accessors: if the GE backtest file is missing or schema-shifted,
// we drop the headline numbers and show a pointer to the full backtest page.
const backtestRows = Array.isArray(b?.rows) ? b.rows.length : null;
const unsAcc = typeof b?.summary?.uns?.winner_accuracy === "number" ? b.summary.uns.winner_accuracy : null;
const unsMae = typeof b?.summary?.uns?.major_party_mae_avg === "number" ? b.summary.uns.major_party_mae_avg : null;
const stmAcc = typeof b?.summary?.stm?.winner_accuracy === "number" ? b.summary.stm.winner_accuracy : null;
const stmMae = typeof b?.summary?.stm?.major_party_mae_avg === "number" ? b.summary.stm.major_party_mae_avg : null;
const stmLift = typeof b?.summary?.stm_lift_over_uns_pp === "number" ? b.summary.stm_lift_over_uns_pp : null;
const hasBacktestFigures =
  backtestRows !== null && unsAcc !== null && unsMae !== null && stmAcc !== null && stmMae !== null;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="General election forecast, methodology"
  description="How the ukelections.co.uk general election constituency forecast is built: Strong Transition Model swing, BES priors, sitting-MP layer, tactical voting, by-election overlay, win probabilities and seat ranges."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methodology</p>
    <h1>How we forecast 650 constituencies</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Strong Transition Model swing, BES Wave 1-30 demographic priors,
      sitting-MP layer, Curtice/Fisher tactical voting overlay, recent by-election
      overlay, then a win probability and a seat range fitted on the 2024 rerun.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Pipeline</p>
      <h2>Composition order</h2>
    </div>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>GE2024 baseline.</strong> Each constituency's actual result on 4 July 2024, taken from Democracy Club CC0 data + Returning Officer declarations. 650/650 constituencies.</li>
      <li><strong>National swing. Strong Transition Model (STM).</strong> Replaces additive Uniform National Swing. Losers shed share locally in proportion to their local vote; gainers absorb the freed share pro-rata to national gain. Bounded in [0,1] by construction. Reference: <a href="https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/blogs/ec_mrpinfo_20240604.html">Electoral Calculus / Baxter STM</a>. <strong>+1.0pp MAE lift over UNS</strong> on the GE2024 backtest.</li>
      <li><strong>BES regional/demographic prior.</strong> 36,416 post-GE2024 respondents from the British Election Study Wave 1-30 panel, post-stratified into (region × age × ethnicity × tenure) cells with Dirichlet pooling κ=15, then projected to LAD-level priors. Each constituency takes its own constituency-level prior where one exists, otherwise the average of the priors for the local authorities it overlaps, otherwise its region's; the prior is blended in at {pct0(params.bes_prior_weight)} weight.</li>
      <li><strong>Sitting-MP layer.</strong> Personal-vote bonus for incumbents standing again (+1pp at &lt;5yr → +4pp at ≥20yr tenure); retirement drag for standing-down MPs (-2pp to -3pp); open-seat treatment for defectors / suspended MPs.</li>
      <li><strong>Tactical voting overlay.</strong> Curtice/Fisher Lab/LD/Green progressive squeeze. When 1st = Conservative or Reform with ≤10pp lead over a progressive 2nd, transfer {pct0(params.tactical_voting_transfer_rate)} of a 3rd-placed progressive's share to the 2nd-placed.</li>
      <li><strong>English-identity floor (Census 2021 TS027).</strong> Reform UK gets a small lift in constituencies where the share of residents giving an "English only" or "English and British" national identity is high. +1pp at 22%+, +2pp at 30%+, +3pp at 40%+. Frontiers (2025) "drivers of Reform UK support" finds English national identity is the single strongest demographic predictor of Reform vote (standardised β ≈ 0.45).</li>
      <li><strong>Age-structure adjustment (Census 2021 TS007A).</strong> Reform and Conservative get a small lift in constituencies with a high 65+ share. Dampened to 40% strength when a BES prior is already in play, since BES respondents implicitly carry the age signal.</li>
      <li><strong>Reform Muslim-share ceiling.</strong> Reform's predicted share is capped at the demographically-plausible ceiling (12% if the constituency is &gt;50% Muslim, 22% if 30-50%, 30% if 15-30%). Excess is redistributed pro-rata. Calibrated against Sobolewska &amp; Ford (Brexitland, 2020).</li>
      <li><strong>Independent ceiling.</strong> Independent vote capped at 8% in regular contests. The cap is bypassed for known high-profile independents (Galloway, Corbyn, Lloyd etc.).</li>
      <li><strong>By-election overlay.</strong> Where a post-GE2024 by-election has been held in this constituency, blend {pct0(params.by_election_overlay_weight)} of the by-election's shares into the prediction.</li>
      <li><strong>Normalise.</strong> Re-scale shares to sum to 1.0.</li>
      <li><strong>Win probability, share range and seat range.</strong> See below.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  {wp && (
    <section class="section" id="uncertainty">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">Uncertainty</p>
        <h2>Win probability, share ranges and seat ranges</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
        <strong>Win probability.</strong> Each seat's figure is the share of seats in our 2024 rerun with a similar
        predicted margin between first and second place that the projected leader went on to win. The rerun swings
        the 2019 result to the May 2024 polling average and scores it against 4 July 2024, {wp.n} seats. Fitted
        {" "}{wp.fitted_at?.slice(0, 10)} by <code>scripts/calibrate-ge-win-probability.mjs</code> into
        {" "}<code>data/calibration/ge-win-probability.json</code>. On regions it never saw it scores a Brier of
        {" "}{wp.validation.brier.toFixed(3)} against {wp.validation.flat_baseline_brier.toFixed(3)} for quoting one
        flat rate everywhere. The <a href="/data-quality/ge-backtest/#reliability">reliability table</a> is on the backtest page.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Share range.</strong> The low and high figure for each party (p10 and p90) are drawn from the same
        rerun's errors, party by party. On held-out regions the actual 2024 share fell inside that range
        {" "}{Math.round(wp.interval_coverage.overall * 100)} times in 100, against the 80 the range claims.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Seat range.</strong> {geSummary.seat_ranges?.method} The published range is the
        {" "}{geSummary.seat_ranges?.interval}, from {geSummary.seat_ranges?.draws?.toLocaleString()} draws, with
        {" "}rho {geSummary.seat_ranges?.rho}. {geSummary.seat_ranges?.limits}
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What these do not include.</strong> {wp.limits?.join(" ")}
      </p>
    </section>
  )}

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Backtest</p>
      <h2>How the model performs on GE2024</h2>
    </div>
    {hasBacktestFigures ? (
      <>
        <p>
          Predicting GE2024 from the GE2019 result (notional 2019 results where boundaries changed), fed the actual
          2024 national vote shares, evaluated on <strong>{backtestRows!.toLocaleString()}</strong> constituencies.
          This tests how the model spreads a known national result across seats; the run fed the May 2024 polling
          average instead is on the <a href="/data-quality/ge-backtest/">backtest page</a>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Uniform National Swing baseline: <strong>{(unsAcc! * 100).toFixed(1)}%</strong> winner accuracy, <strong>{(unsMae! * 100).toFixed(2)}pp</strong> major-party MAE.</li>
          <li>
            Strong Transition Model: <strong>{(stmAcc! * 100).toFixed(1)}%</strong> winner accuracy, <strong>{(stmMae! * 100).toFixed(2)}pp</strong> major-party MAE
            {stmLift !== null ? <>, a <strong>{stmLift.toFixed(2)}pp</strong> lift over UNS.</> : "."}
          </li>
        </ul>
      </>
    ) : (
      <p>
        Backtest figures are regenerated each pipeline run; the full tables live at
        <a href="/data-quality/ge-backtest/">/data-quality/ge-backtest/</a>.
      </p>
    )}
    <p class="muted-row">
      The file's own targets are {Math.round((b?.snapshot?.target_metrics?.winner_accuracy_target ?? 0) * 100)}% of winners
      and {b?.snapshot?.target_metrics?.major_party_mae_target_pp}pp major-party error; neither run meets them.
    </p>
    <p class="muted-row">
      <a href="/data-quality/ge-backtest/">Full backtest tables</a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Assumptions log</p>
      <h2>Every parameter, version-controlled</h2>
    </div>
    <p>
      The weights the run passes to the model are written to
      <code>data/predictions/ge-next/assumptions.json</code> from the same options object, and a test fails the
      build if the two ever differ. The nightly refresh does not pre-register each run; the forecast files were last
      hashed in the 26 April 2026 pre-registration (<code>data/transparency/preregistration-2026-04-26.json</code>).
      Current values:
    </p>
    <pre class="assumptions-json">{JSON.stringify(a.parameters, null, 2)}</pre>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="section-head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Academic references</p>
      <h2>Sources</h2>
    </div>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="https://benjaminlauderdale.net/files/papers/mrp-polling-paper.pdf">Lauderdale, Bailey, Blumenau, Rivers (2020)</a>, foundational MRP paper</li>
      <li><a href="https://benjaminlauderdale.net/files/papers/2016HanrettyLauderdaleVivyanPSRM.pdf">Hanretty, Lauderdale, Vivyan (2018)</a>, comparison of MRP strategies</li>
      <li><a href="https://yougov.co.uk/politics/articles/49968-how-yougovs-seat-and-vote-projections-fared-at-the-2024-uk-general-election">YouGov 2024 fared paper</a>, unwinding correction</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.britishpollingcouncil.org/wordpress/wp-content/uploads/2025/04/ge24-survation-mrp-postmortem.pdf">Survation/Hanretty 2024 MRP post-mortem</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.focaldata.com/blog/review-into-our-performance-at-the-2024-uk-general-election">Focaldata 2024 review</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://www.electoralcalculus.co.uk/blogs/ec_mrpinfo_20240604.html">Electoral Calculus Strong Transition Model</a></li>
      <li><a href="https://users.ox.ac.uk/~nuff0084/FisherTT.pdf">Fisher (2004), tactical voting and tactical non-voting</a></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <p class="muted-row">
      <a href="/forecasts/general-election/">National forecast</a> ·
      <a href="/transparency/">Transparency &amp; pre-registration</a> ·
      <a href="/data-quality/ge-backtest/">Backtest details</a>
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .assumptions-json {
    white-space: pre-wrap;
    background: var(--surface-muted);
    padding: var(--space-md);
  }
</style>
